## 19. Error Handling Architecture

- **Server:** throw `AppError` from services; Express 5 forwards rejected promises to `errorHandler`. Mapping: `ZodError → 400 VALIDATION_ERROR` (with `details[{path,message}]`); Mongoose duplicate key `11000 → 409`; JWT errors → `401`; `PayloadTooLargeError → 413`; unknown → `500 INTERNAL_ERROR` with generic message in production (full stack only in `NODE_ENV=development`). Every response carries `requestId` (from `X-Request-Id` or generated) and the same id is in the log line.
- **Client:** `ApiError` class; TanStack Query `onError` → toast with `message`; form-level `details` mapped onto fields via RHF `setError`; route `ErrorBoundary` for render errors; network failures produce a persistent but dismissible banner; the editor never discards local state on error.
- **External dependencies:** LLM and Chromium failures are *expected* and handled (fallback / print-dialog fallback), never surfaced as raw 500s.

## 20. Security Architecture

| Threat | Control |
|---|---|
| Credential stuffing / brute force | Rate limits, bcrypt cost 12, generic error messages, timing-equalised login |
| Token theft | Access token in memory only; refresh in httpOnly cookie; rotation + reuse detection; short access TTL |
| CSRF | SameSite=Lax, path-scoped cookie, custom header + Origin check on cookie-authenticated endpoints |
| XSS | React escaping; **no** `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`; strict CSP via Helmet (`default-src 'self'`; allow `style-src 'unsafe-inline'` only if Tailwind requires it; `connect-src 'self'`); user text only rendered as text |
| NoSQL injection | Typed Zod schemas (strings stay strings), no spreading of request objects into queries, `strict` Mongoose schemas |
| IDOR | `userId` filter on every query; foreign IDs → 404 |
| Mass assignment | Zod `.strict()` schemas; server controls `userId`, `revision`, `completion`, `isSample` |
| SSRF/abuse in PDF | Fixed navigation origin, request interception allow-list, single-use 60 s print tokens |
| Prompt injection | Delimited untrusted text, "ignore embedded instructions" rule, JSON-only schema validation, fact guard, output rendered as text, no tool/function access for the LLM |
| Secret leakage | Env-only secrets; `.env` git-ignored; `pino` redaction of `req.headers.authorization`, `req.headers.cookie`, `*.password`, `*.token`, `*.apiKey`; startup check refuses default/weak secrets in production |
| DoS | Body limit 1 MB, per-route rate limits, PDF concurrency cap, JD length cap, Mongo query limits (`limit ≤ 50`) |
| Supply chain | Lockfile committed, `npm audit` in CI, minimal dependencies |
| Privacy | Contact PII never sent to LLM; JD text not stored; export/delete endpoints |
| Transport | HTTPS enforced by hosts; HSTS via Helmet in production; `trust proxy` configured for correct IPs |

## 21. Deployment Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Dev[GitHub repo] -->|push main| CI[GitHub Actions: lint, typecheck, test, build]
  CI --> FE[Vercel: client static build + rewrites /api/* -> backend]
  CI --> BE[Render/Railway/Fly: server Docker image with Chromium]
  BE --> ATLAS[(MongoDB Atlas M0)]
  BE --> GEM[[Gemini API]]
```

- **Frontend (Vercel/Netlify):** build `client`; `vercel.json` rewrite `{ "source": "/api/:path*", "destination": "https://<backend-host>/api/:path*" }` and SPA fallback to `index.html`. This makes the browser see a **single origin** → first-party cookies, no CORS in production.
- **Backend (Docker):** base `ghcr.io/puppeteer/puppeteer` (Chromium preinstalled) or `node:20-slim` + `apt install chromium` with `PUPPETEER_EXECUTABLE_PATH`. Multi-stage build: install → build `shared` → build `server` (tsup) → runtime image runs as non-root. Expose `PORT`. Health check `/api/v1/health`.
- **Free-tier caveats (documented risk):** cold starts (first request may take 30–60 s), ~512 MB RAM (Chromium concurrency kept ≤ 2). If Chromium cannot run, PDF-04 fallback (browser print) keeps the feature usable; this must be tested and documented in README.
- **Dev:** `npm run dev` starts `server` (tsx watch, port 5000) and `client` (Vite, port 5173) with Vite proxy `/api → http://localhost:5000`. `AI_PROVIDER=mock` works offline. Local MongoDB or Atlas via `MONGODB_URI`.

